Step 1: Create a New Repository on GitHub
Log in to your GitHub account.
Click the + icon in the top-right and select New repository.
Name your repository (e.g., my-project). To avoid conflicts, do not initialize it with a README or .gitignore.
Click Create repository and copy the HTTPS URL provided (e.g., https://github.com).

Step 2: Initialize and Commit Locally
Open your terminal (CMD or Git Bash) in your project folder and run:
Initialize Git: git init -b main (this creates a hidden .git folder).
Stage Files: git add . (prepares all files for the first commit).
Commit Changes: git commit -m "Initial commit".

