Fetch latest branch using "git fetch"

1. Setup & Configuration
Before starting, set your identity to ensure your contributions are correctly attributed.

git config --global user.name "Your Name": Sets your name for all commits.
git config --global user.email "your_email@example.com": Sets your email for all commits.
git config --list: Displays all your current Git settings.

2. Starting a Project
git init: Initializes a new, empty Git repository in your current folder.
git clone [URL]: Downloads an existing repository and its entire history from a remote server.

3. The Core Workflow (Stage & Commit)
This is the standard cycle for saving your work locally.

git status: Shows the current state of your files (modified, staged, or untracked).
git add [file]: Moves a specific file to the staging area.
git add .: Stages all modified and new files at once.
git commit -m "your message": Saves your staged snapshot to the local repository with a descriptive message.
git diff: Shows line-by-line differences for files that have not yet been staged.

4. Branching & Merging
Branches allow you to work on new features without affecting the main code.

git branch: Lists all local branches in your repository.
git branch [branch-name]: Creates a new branch with the specified name.
git checkout [branch-name]: Switches to the specified branch.
git checkout -b [branch-name]: Combines creating and switching to a new branch in one step.
git merge [branch-name]: Integrates changes from the specified branch into your current branch.

5. Remote Syncing
These commands connect your local work to servers like GitHub or GitLab.
git remote add origin [URL]: Connects your local repository to a remote server.
git push -u origin [branch-name]: Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.
git pull: Downloads changes from the remote repository and immediately merges them into your current branch.
git fetch: Downloads updates from the remote repo without merging them, allowing you to review them first.

6. Undoing & Inspecting Changes
git log: Displays the commit history for the current branch.
git log --oneline: Shows a compact, one-line-per-commit history.
git stash: Temporarily saves uncommitted changes so you can switch branches without committing.
git stash pop: Restores the most recently stashed changes.
git reset --hard HEAD: Discards all local changes and reverts to the last commit (use with caution).
git revert [commit-ID]: Creates a new commit that undoes the changes of a previous one without deleting its history








